      "id": 1,
      "text": "Belleview began in 1815 as Mount Vernon, later renamed Belleview for its scenic river views or a local bell.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/belleview_kentucky",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "By the 1860s Belleview boasted two tobacco warehouses, a hotel, several stores, and a willow basket factory that served river traffic.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/belleview_kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The relocated Grant Post Office building, built around 1880, sits on the National Register of Historic Places since 1990.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kyatlas.com/ky-belleview.html",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Belleview was severely flooded during the 1937 Ohio River flood, a watershed event that reshaped the town's commerce.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/belleview_kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Just seven miles west lies Big Bone Lick State Historic Site, a paleontological treasure preserving prehistoric mammal fossils.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/belleview_kentucky",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Ohio River forms Belleview's northern boundary, its dynamic hydrology shaping the town's alluvial soils and wetlands.",
